Back to numbers between 0-99, let's try my idea:
char nb;
char n10;
char n1;
n10 = nb / 10 + '0;
write(1, &n10, 1);
n1 = nb % 10 + '0';
write(1, &n1, 1);

Wrong.
1. The function needs to be int, not char, char can only restore 1 character, too limited to restore something bigger.
2. missing a ' for '0'
3. only words for 10-99
  // if it's 7 for example, it will print 07, adding a 0

void  ft_putnbr(int nb)
{
  char n10;
  char n1;

  if (nb >= 10)
  {
  n10 = nb / 10 + '0';
  write(1, &n10, 1);
  }
  
  n1 = nb % 10 + '0';
  write(1, &n1, 1);
}
This one works for 0-99 only.

